%% ============================================================
%% 20.1 Introduction
%% ============================================================
\section{Introduction}\label{sec:type-systems-intro}

A type system is a static analysis that classifies program phrases by the
kinds of values they compute, with the goal of preventing certain kinds of
runtime errors.  It is arguably the most important static analysis in any
programming language: it provides the primary guarantee that well-typed
programs ``do not go wrong.''

This chapter provides a comprehensive overview of type systems.  We begin
with the fundamental question: what is a type?  We then develop the theory
of type safety (progress and preservation), discuss soundness proofs, and
examine type systems as static analyses.  We survey the landscape of type
systems---from simple types through polymorphism, subtyping, and dependent
types---and discuss the decidability of type checking.

\begin{definition}[Type System]\label{def:type-system}
  A \emph{type system} is a set of rules that assign types to the
  constructs of a programming language.  Formally, it is a decidable
  relation $\Gamma \vdash e : \tau$ (``in context $\Gamma$, expression $e$
  has type $\tau$'') that is sound with respect to the language's semantics.
\end{definition}

%% ============================================================
%% 20.2 What Is a Type?
%% ============================================================
\section{What Is a Type?}\label{sec:what-is-type}

The question ``what is a type?'' has no single answer; different
perspectives illuminate different aspects.

\subsection{Types as Classifications}\label{sec:types-classifications}

\begin{definition}[Type as Classification]\label{def:type-classification}
  A \emph{type} is a classification of values.  The type $\Int$ classifies
  integer values; the type $\Bool$ classifies boolean values; the type
  $\alpha \to \beta$ classifies functions from $\alpha$ to $\beta$.
\end{definition}

This is the most intuitive view: a type tells you what kind of value an
expression produces.  It corresponds to the set-theoretic notion of a set
of values.

\subsection{Types as Interfaces}\label{sec:types-interfaces}

\begin{definition}[Type as Interface]\label{def:type-interface}
  A \emph{type} describes the operations that can be performed on a value,
  without revealing its representation.  The type $\texttt{Stack}$ tells
  you that you can push, pop, and check emptiness, but not how the stack
  is implemented.
\end{definition}

This view emphasizes information hiding: types are contracts between the
provider and the consumer of a value.

\subsection{Types as Properties}\label{sec:types-properties}

\begin{definition}[Type as Property]\label{def:type-property}
  A \emph{type} is a property of programs that can be checked statically.
  Under this view, type checking is a form of \emph{model checking}: we
  verify that the program satisfies certain properties before it runs.
\end{definition}

This view is most natural in the context of dependent types and refinement
types, where types can express arbitrary properties of programs.

\subsection{Types as Games}\label{sec:types-games}

In game semantics, types are interpreted as games between two players
(Proponent and Opponent), and programs are strategies for playing the game.
This provides a deep connection between types, logic, and computation.

\begin{insight}
The different views of types---classifications, interfaces, properties,
games---are not contradictory but complementary.  Each illuminates a
different aspect of what types do and why they matter.  A complete
understanding of type systems requires all of these perspectives.
\end{insight}

%% ============================================================
%% 20.3 Type Safety: Progress and Preservation
%% ============================================================
\section{Type Safety: Progress and Preservation}\label{sec:type-safety}

The fundamental theorem of type safety states that well-typed programs do
not go wrong.  This is formalized through two properties: progress and
preservation.

\subsection{The Progress Theorem}\label{sec:progress}

\begin{theorem}[Progress]\label{thm:progress}
  If $\vdash e : \tau$ then either $e$ is a value, or there exists $e'$
  such that $e \stepsto e'$.
\end{theorem}

Progress says that a well-typed expression is never \emph{stuck}: it can
always make progress (either it is already a value, or it can take a step).
A \emph{stuck} state is one that is neither a value nor can take a step---
this is what we mean by ``going wrong.''

\begin{definition}[Stuck State]\label{def:stuck}
  An expression $e$ is \emph{stuck} if it is not a value and there is no
  $e'$ such that $e \stepsto e'$.
\end{definition}

\subsection{The Preservation Theorem}\label{sec:preservation}

\begin{theorem}[Preservation (Subject Reduction)]\label{thm:preservation}
  If $\vdash e : \tau$ and $e \stepsto e'$ then $\vdash e' : \tau$.
\end{theorem}

Preservation says that evaluation preserves types: if an expression has
type $\tau$ before a step, it still has type $\tau$ after the step.

\subsection{Type Soundness}\label{sec:type-soundness}

\begin{theorem}[Type Soundness]\label{thm:type-soundness}
  If $\vdash e : \tau$ then either:
  \begin{enumerate}
    \item $e \multistepsto v$ for some value $v$ with $\vdash v : \tau$, or
    \item $e \multistepsto e'$ and $e' \multistepsto e' \multistepsto \ldots$ (divergence).
  \end{enumerate}
  In particular, $e$ never reaches a stuck state.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
  By induction on the number of steps.  Progress ensures that a well-typed
  expression is never stuck.  Preservation ensures that after each step,
  the expression is still well-typed.  Therefore, by induction, the
  expression is never stuck after any number of steps.
\end{proof}

\begin{figure}[htbp]
  \centering
  \begin{tikzpicture}[>=stealth, node distance=1.5cm]
    \node[circle, draw, fill=green!20] (e) {$e : \tau$};
    \node[circle, draw, fill=green!20, right=2cm of e] (e1) {$e' : \tau$};
    \node[circle, draw, fill=green!20, right=2cm of e1] (e2) {$e'' : \tau$};
    \node[circle, draw, fill=blue!20, right=2cm of e2] (v) {$v : \tau$};
    
    \draw[->] (e) -- node[above] {step} (e1);
    \draw[->] (e1) -- node[above] {step} (e2);
    \draw[->, dashed] (e2) -- node[above] {steps$^*$} (v);
    
    \node[below=0.5cm of e] {well-typed};
    \node[below=0.5cm of e1] {well-typed};
    \node[below=0.5cm of e2] {well-typed};
    \node[below=0.5cm of v] {value};
  \end{tikzpicture}
  \caption{Type soundness: well-typed expressions stay well-typed and
    eventually reach a value (or diverge).}
  \label{fig:type-soundness}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Examples of Type-Unsafe Languages}\label{sec:type-unsafe}

\begin{example}[Type Errors in C]\label{ex:type-unsafe-c}
  C is not type safe:
  \begin{verbatim}
  int x = 42;
  int *p = &x;
  float *q = (float *)p;  // cast bypasses type system
  float f = *q;            // interprets int bits as float
  \end{verbatim}
  The cast $(\texttt{float *})$ allows the programmer to bypass the type
  system, leading to undefined behavior.
\end{example}

%% ============================================================
%% 20.4 Soundness Proofs
%% ============================================================
\section{Soundness Proofs}\label{sec:soundness-proofs}

Proving type soundness is a central activity in programming language
research.  We outline the proof technique here.

\subsection{Proof Strategy}\label{sec:proof-strategy}

The standard proof of type soundness proceeds in two parts:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Progress}: By induction on the typing derivation
    $\vdash e : \tau$.
  \item \textbf{Preservation}: By induction on the step $e \stepsto e'$.
\end{enumerate}

Both proofs require a \emph{canonical forms lemma}:

\begin{lemma}[Canonical Forms]\label{lem:canonical-forms}
  \begin{enumerate}
    \item If $\vdash v : \Int$ then $v$ is an integer literal.
    \item If $\vdash v : \Bool$ then $v$ is $\mathbf{true}$ or $\mathbf{false}$.
    \item If $\vdash v : \tau_1 \to \tau_2$ then $v = \lambda x.\, e$ for
      some $x$ and $e$ with $x:\tau_1 \vdash e : \tau_2$.
  \end{enumerate}
\end{lemma}

\subsection{Proof of Progress}\label{sec:proof-progress}

\begin{proof}[Proof of Progress (\Cref{thm:progress})]
  By induction on the derivation of $\vdash e : \tau$.
  
  \textbf{Case} $e = n$ (integer literal): $e$ is a value.
  
  \textbf{Case} $e = e_1 + e_2$: By the typing rule, $\vdash e_1 : \Int$
  and $\vdash e_2 : \Int$.  By the induction hypothesis, either $e_1$ is a
  value or $e_1 \stepsto e_1'$.  If $e_1$ is not a value, then
  $e_1 + e_2 \stepsto e_1' + e_2$.  If $e_1$ is a value, we apply the same
  reasoning to $e_2$.  If both are values, by the canonical forms lemma,
  they are integer literals, and $e_1 + e_2 \stepsto n$ for some $n$.
  
  \textbf{Case} $e = e_1\ e_2$: By the typing rule, $\vdash e_1 : \tau_2 \to \tau$
  and $\vdash e_2 : \tau_2$.  By induction, either $e_1$ steps or is a
  value.  If $e_1$ is a value, by canonical forms, $e_1 = \lambda x.\, e$.
  Then $e_1\ e_2 \stepsto e[e_2/x]$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Proof of Preservation}\label{sec:proof-preservation}

\begin{proof}[Proof of Preservation (\Cref{thm:preservation})]
  By induction on the derivation of $e \stepsto e'$.
  
  \textbf{Case} $\beta$-reduction: $(\lambda x.\, e)\, v \stepsto e[v/x]$.
  By the typing rule, $x:\tau_2 \vdash e : \tau$ and $\vdash v : \tau_2$.
  By the substitution lemma, $\vdash e[v/x] : \tau$.
  
  \textbf{Case} Congruence: $e_1 \stepsto e_1'$ implies $e_1 + e_2 \stepsto e_1' + e_2$.
  By induction, $\vdash e_1' : \Int$.  By the typing rule,
  $\vdash e_1' + e_2 : \Int$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The Substitution Lemma}\label{sec:substitution-lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Substitution]\label{lem:substitution}
  If $\Gamma, x:\sigma \vdash e : \tau$ and $\vdash v : \sigma$ then
  $\Gamma \vdash e[v/x] : \tau$.
\end{lemma}

The substitution lemma is essential for proving preservation of
$\beta$-reduction.  It states that substituting a well-typed value for a
variable preserves the typing of the expression.

%% ============================================================
%% 20.5 Type Systems as Static Analyses
%% ============================================================
\section{Type Systems as Static Analyses}\label{sec:types-as-analyses}

\subsection{Type Systems and Abstract Interpretation}\label{sec:types-abstract}

\begin{definition}[Type System as Analysis]\label{def:type-analysis}
  A type system can be viewed as a \emph{static analysis} that maps each
  program point to an approximation of the set of values it may produce.
  The type $\tau$ approximates the set of values $\{v \mid \vdash v : \tau\}$.
\end{definition}

From the perspective of abstract interpretation:
\begin{itemize}
  \item The \emph{concrete domain} is the set of all program behaviors.
  \item The \emph{abstract domain} is the set of types.
  \item The \emph{abstraction function} maps a set of values to their
    common type.
  \item The \emph{concretization function} maps a type to the set of values
    it classifies.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Type Systems vs. Other Analyses}\label{sec:types-vs-analyses}

\begin{table}[htbp]
  \centering
  \caption{Type Systems vs. Other Static Analyses}
  \label{tab:types-vs-analyses}
  \begin{tabular}{llll}
    \hline
    \textbf{Analysis} & \textbf{Property} & \textbf{Enforced} & \textbf{Precision} \\
    \hline
    Type system & No type errors & Yes (compile error) & Low \\
    Null analysis & No null dereferences & Optional & Medium \\
    Bounds analysis & No out-of-bounds & Optional & Medium \\
    Effect system & No unexpected effects & Optional & High \\
    Termination & Always terminates & Optional (total langs) & Low \\
    \hline
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Type Inference as Analysis}\label{sec:type-inference-analysis}

Type inference can be viewed as a static analysis that infers the most
precise type for each expression:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Hindley--Milner}: Infers the principal (most general) type.
  \item \textbf{Local type inference}: Infers types locally, requiring
    annotations at certain points (e.g., function parameters).
  \item \textbf{Bidirectional type checking}: Combines type checking
    (top-down) and type inference (bottom-up).
\end{itemize}

%% ============================================================
%% 20.6 Decidability of Type Checking
%% ============================================================
\section{Decidability of Type Checking}\label{sec:decidability}

\subsection{Decidable Type Systems}\label{sec:decidable-types}

\begin{definition}[Decidable Type Checking]\label{def:decidable-type-checking}
  A type system is \emph{decidable} if there exists an algorithm that,
  given any context $\Gamma$, expression $e$, and type $\tau$, determines
  whether $\Gamma \vdash e : \tau$ in finite time.
\end{definition}

Many commonly used type systems are decidable:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Simple types (STLC): $O(n)$ with annotations.
  \item Hindley--Milner: $O(n^2)$ worst case, nearly linear in practice.
  \item System F (with annotations): decidable.
  \item Subtyping with bounded quantification (F$_{<:}$): decidable for
    certain variants.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Undecidable Type Systems}\label{sec:undecidable-types}

Some type systems are undecidable:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{System F type inference}: Given $e$, does there exist $\tau$
    such that $\vdash e : \tau$?  Undecidable~\cite{wells1999typability}.
  \item \textbf{Dependent type checking with general recursion}: If the
    language allows non-terminating terms, type checking is undecidable.
  \item \textbf{C++ template metaprogramming}: Template instantiation is
    Turing-complete, so type checking is undecidable.
  \item \textbf{Type inference with subtyping}: In some configurations,
    type inference with subtyping is undecidable.
\end{itemize}

\begin{theorem}[Girard's Paradox]\label{thm:girard}
  System U (a type theory with $\texttt{Type} : \texttt{Type}$) is
  inconsistent.  This shows that certain ``natural'' type systems are
  unsound.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Practical Implications}\label{sec:decidability-practical}

The decidability of type checking has practical implications:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Compiler termination}: A decidable type system guarantees
    that the compiler will terminate.
  \item \textbf{Error messages}: Decidable type systems can always produce
    a yes/no answer, which simplifies error reporting.
  \item \textbf{Language design}: Designers must ensure that new features
    do not make type checking undecidable.
\end{itemize}

\begin{insight}
The tension between expressiveness and decidability is a recurring theme in
type system design.  Every increase in the power of the type system risks
making type checking undecidable.  The art lies in finding the sweet spot:
a type system that is expressive enough to be useful but decidable enough
to be practical.
\end{insight}

%% ============================================================
%% 20.7 Survey of Type System Features
%% ============================================================
\section{Survey of Type System Features}\label{sec:type-survey}

\subsection{Simple Types}\label{sec:simple-types}

The simply-typed lambda calculus (\Cref{ch:stlc}) provides the foundation:

\begin{grammar}{Simple Types}
  \production{\tau}{\alpha \alt \tau_1 \to \tau_2 \alt \tau_1 \times \tau_2 \alt \tau_1 + \tau_2 \alt \Int \alt \Bool \alt \mathbf{1}}
\end{grammar}

\begin{typerules}{Simply Typed Lambda Calculus}
  \typeaxiom{\textsc{Var}}{
    \dfrac{}{\Gamma, x:\tau \vdash x : \tau}
  } \\[8pt]
  \typerule{\textsc{Abs}}{
    \Gamma, x:\tau_1 \vdash e : \tau_2
  }{
    \Gamma \vdash \lambda x:\tau_1.\, e : \tau_1 \to \tau_2
  } \\[8pt]
  \typerule{\textsc{App}}{
    \Gamma \vdash e_1 : \tau_2 \to \tau \quad
    \Gamma \vdash e_2 : \tau_2
  }{
    \Gamma \vdash e_1\ e_2 : \tau
  }
\end{typerules}

\subsection{Polymorphism}\label{sec:polymorphism-overview}

Polymorphism allows functions to operate on values of multiple types.
Three forms are distinguished:

\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Parametric polymorphism} (System F, ML, Haskell): Functions
    are parameterized by types.  $\Lambda \alpha.\, \lambda x:\alpha.\, x$
    works for all types $\alpha$.
  \item \textbf{Ad hoc polymorphism} (overloading, type classes): Functions
    have different implementations for different types.
  \item \textbf{Subtype polymorphism} (OOP): Functions operate on a type
    and all its subtypes.
\end{enumerate}

We cover polymorphism in depth in \Cref{ch:polymorphism}.

\subsection{Subtyping}\label{sec:subtyping-overview}

Subtyping allows a value of one type to be used where another type is
expected:

\begin{definition}[Subtyping]\label{def:subtyping}
  If $\tau_1 <: \tau_2$ (read ``$\tau_1$ is a subtype of $\tau_2$''), then
  any value of type $\tau_1$ can be used where a value of type $\tau_2$ is
  expected.  This is the \emph{subsumption rule}:
  \[
    \dfrac{\Gamma \vdash e : \tau_1 \quad \tau_1 <: \tau_2}{\Gamma \vdash e : \tau_2}
    \;\textsc{Sub}
  \]
\end{definition}

\subsection{Dependent Types}\label{sec:dependent-types-overview}

Dependent types allow types to depend on values:

\begin{definition}[Dependent Type]\label{def:dependent-type}
  A \emph{dependent type} is a type that contains a value as a component.
  For example, $\texttt{Vec}\, n\, \alpha$ is the type of vectors of length
  $n$ with elements of type $\alpha$.  The length $n$ is a value that
  appears in the type.
\end{definition}

Dependent types are covered in depth in \Cref{ch:dependent-types}.

\subsection{Linear and Affine Types}\label{sec:linear-types}

\begin{definition}[Linear Type]\label{def:linear-type}
  A \emph{linear type} is a type whose values must be used exactly once.
  This prevents resource leaks (use zero times) and double-frees (use more
  than once).
\end{definition}

Linear types are used in Rust (ownership), session types (protocol
compliance), and quantum computing (no-cloning theorem).

\subsection{Effect Types}\label{sec:effect-types}

\begin{definition}[Effect Type]\label{def:effect-type}
  An \emph{effect type} tracks the computational effects that an expression
  may perform: exceptions, state mutation, I/O, etc.
\end{definition}

Effect types are used in Koka, Eff, and Unison to provide fine-grained
control over side effects.

\subsection{Summary of Type System Features}\label{sec:type-summary-table}

\begin{table}[htbp]
  \centering
  \caption{Type System Feature Summary}
  \label{tab:type-features}
  \begin{tabular}{llll}
    \hline
    \textbf{Feature} & \textbf{Example Languages} & \textbf{Decidable} & \textbf{Inference} \\
    \hline
    Simple types & OCaml, Rust & Yes & Yes \\
    Parametric poly. & ML, Haskell, Rust & Yes & Yes (HM) \\
    Subtyping & Java, C\#, Scala & Yes & Partial \\
    Dependent types & Agda, Idris, Lean & Semi-decidable & Partial \\
    Linear types & Rust, Linear Haskell & Yes & Partial \\
    Effect types & Koka, Eff & Yes & Yes \\
    Gradual types & TypeScript, Pyright & Yes & Yes \\
    \hline
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

%% ============================================================
%% 20.8 Summary
%% ============================================================
\section{Summary}\label{sec:type-systems-summary}

Type systems are the cornerstone of programming language design.  They
provide the primary static guarantee that programs are ``correct'' in the
sense that they do not exhibit certain classes of errors at runtime.

The theory of type safety---progress and preservation---provides a
principled way to prove that a type system is sound.  The proof technique
is now standard and has been applied to hundreds of type systems.

The landscape of type systems is vast: from simple types through
polymorphism, subtyping, dependent types, linear types, and effect types.
Each feature increases the expressiveness of the type system but also
increases the complexity of type checking.  The challenge for language
designers is to find the right combination of features for their
application domain.

In the following chapters, we explore specific type system features in
depth: polymorphism (\Cref{ch:polymorphism}) and gradual typing
(\Cref{ch:gradual-typing}).
